WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 287: John Muir: The Range of Light, Yosemite, Theodore Roosevelt, and the Sierra Club

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.760
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:17.920
[Rebecca] Today, we conclude our landmark top 50 famous non-president American series with the bearded wilderness prophet who taught a developing nation to revere the majesty of the wild.

4
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:24.160
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary life of John Muir.

5
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:42.000
[George] Born in April 1838 in the coastal Scottish port of Dunbar, John Muir grew up under the austere, unrelenting discipline of his deeply religious father Daniel, who forced young John to memorize the entire New Testament and most of the Old Testament under threat of physical beatings.

6
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:51.680
[George] In 1849, the Muir family emigrated to the raw wilderness of Portage, Wisconsin, carving Fountain Lake Farm out of Virgin Oak Savannah.

7
00:00:51.680 --> 00:01:09.040
[George] Rising at one o'clock in the morning in the bitter freezing basement, John displayed an astonishing mechanical genius, inventing intricate wooden clocks that struck alarms, automatic horse feeders, and a mechanical study desk that retrieved and turned the pages of books at timed intervals.

8
00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:16.800
[George] Winning prizes at the Wisconsin State Agricultural Fair, Muir studied geology and botany at the University of Wisconsin.

9
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:29.440
[George] But in March 1867, while working as a machinist in an Indianapolis carriage factory, a metal file slipped, piercing his right eye, with sympathetic blindness striking his left eye.

10
00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:43.680
[George] Confined to a pitch black room for weeks, Muir vowed that if God restored his sight, he would turn his back on mechanical industrial inventions forever and dedicate his life to studying the glorious inventions of God in the living wilderness.

11
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:59.040
[George] Growing up in the coastal fishing village of Dunbar, young John endured the brutal, fundamentalist discipline of his father Daniel, who believed that all worldly amusements were sinful and whipped John daily if he stumbled over a single scripture verse from the Bible.

12
00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:12.600
[George] In the raw Wisconsin woods at Fountain Lake, John escaped his father's harsh tyranny by rising at one o'clock in the morning in the dark cellar, building astonishing mechanical devices from scrap iron and wood by candlelight.

13
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:20.360
[George] His intricate wooden clocks kept perfect astronomical time, and his mechanical study desk was celebrated across the county.

14
00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:36.680
[George] But the terrifying industrial accident in Indianapolis, where a sharp file blinded him in both eyes, was a divine wake-up call that shattered his mechanical ambitions, convincing him that God had spared his sight so he could become a pilgrim of the living wilderness.

15
00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:53.960
[George] When his vision returned miraculously weeks later, 29-year-old Muir packed a small canvas haversack with a copy of Robert Byrne's poems, the New Testament, and William Paley's botany books, setting out on foot from Louisville, Kentucky, in September 1867.

16
00:02:53.960 --> 00:03:07.480
[George] Walking over 1,000 miles across Tennessee, Georgia, and Florida to the Gulf of Mexico, Muir avoided paved roads, walking by compass through Civil War ruins, swamps, and primeval pine forests.

17
00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:15.240
[George] In his journal, published as A Thousand Mile Walk to the Gulf, Muir formulated a radical philosophical revelation.

18
00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:28.280
[George] He rejected the traditional anthropocentric dogma that nature was created solely for human exploitation, declaring that plants, animals, and rocks possessed an inherent sacred right to exist for the glory of their creator.

19
00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:39.240
[George] Stricken with malaria in Florida, Muir boarded a sailing schooner to Cuba and New York, finally boarding a Panama package ship bound for the mythical paradise of California.

20
00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:50.560
[George] Muir's legendary thousand-mile walk through the post-Civil War South was a transformative spiritual pilgrimage that stripped away the conventional prejudices of 19th-century civilization.

21
00:03:50.560 --> 00:04:05.200
[George] Walking through the scorched battlefields of Georgia and the malaria-infested swamps of Florida, Muir slept under open skies in graveyards and oak groves, observing the infinite variety of botanical species with breathless poetic reverence.

22
00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:14.080
[George] In his journal, Muir articulated a revolutionary biocentric philosophy that dismantled the arrogant human conceit of dominion over nature.

23
00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:22.000
[George] He argued that the alligator, the rattlesnake, and the humble thistle possessed an intrinsic sacred value in the eyes of their creator.

24
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:29.280
[George] Existing not for human convenience, but for their own sublime, independent purpose in the grand symphony of life.

25
00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:37.440
[George] Arriving in San Francisco in March 1868, Muir asked an astonished stranger on the wharf the quickest way out of town.

26
00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:43.200
[George] When asked where he wished to go, Muir famously replied, Anywhere that is wild.

27
00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:54.000
[George] Walking across the central valley knee-deep in golden California poppies, Muir beheld the snowcapped crest of the Sierra Nevada for the first time, christening it the Range of Light.

28
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:02.720
[George] Entering Yosemite Valley, Muir was overwhelmed by its sheer granite walls, roaring waterfalls, and towering sequoia groves.

29
00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:22.720
[George] Building a small wooden cabin over a rushing creek near Yosemite Falls, with a small stream running through his living room so he could hear the music of water, Muir spent five years living on dry bread and tea, climbing treacherous peaks without ropes, and reveling in midnight windstorms in the tops of swaying Douglas firs.

30
00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:44.680
[George] When Muir first climbed to the crest of the Pashico Pass in 1868 and gazed across California's central valley toward the shimmering Sierra Nevada, he beheld an earthly paradise, a vast, unbroken carpet of yellow wildflowers stretching to the horizon, bordered by a glowing wall of snowy granite peaks that seemed carved of pure radiance.

31
00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:50.600
[George] Building his famous wooden cabin in Yosemite Valley beneath the roaring thunder of Upper Yosemite Fall.

32
00:05:50.600 --> 00:06:04.680
[George] Muir lived in ecstatic intimacy with the elements, climbing trees in gale force blizzards to ride the bucking boughs and running outside barefoot during the 1872 Inyo earthquake to study the mechanics of granite tailoress avalanches.

33
00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:08.280
[George] Laughing with joy at the living energy of the shaking earth.

34
00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:19.960
[George] At the time, prominent California state geologist Josiah Whitney insisted that Yosemite Valley was formed by a catastrophic earthquake that dropped the canyon floor into the bowels of the earth.

35
00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:35.640
[George] Muir, possessing an intimate, tactile knowledge of every canyon, boldly challenged Whitney's orthodoxy, asserting that the sheer vertical walls of El Capitan and Half Dome were carved over millions of years by the slow, grinding power of colossal glaciers.

36
00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:42.440
[George] Mocked by Whitney as a mere sheepherder and ignoramus, Muir set out into the high country to prove his theory.

37
00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:50.680
[George] Driving wooden stakes into the ice of the McClure Glacier in 1872, Muir measured their precise movement over 46 days.

38
00:06:50.680 --> 00:07:02.040
[George] Discovering living active glaciers in the Sierra and proving his glacial carving hypothesis beyond scientific dispute, earning praise from Louis Agassiz and Ralph Waldo Emerson.

39
00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:14.920
[George] Muir's radical glacial theory challenged the established geological orthodoxy of California state geologist Josiah Whitney, who arrogantly dismissed Muir as an ignorant sheepherder who knew nothing of geological science.

40
00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:29.920
[George] Undeterred, Muir spent months traversing the high Sierra alone, surviving on dry crusts of bread and snow water, mapping glacial moraines, erratic boulders, and polished granite pavements across the high alpine basins.

41
00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:52.240
[George] His discovery of active, living glaciers on the slopes of Mount McClure and Mount Lyell proved that glacial ice had carved the sheer vertical faces of Half Dome, El Capitan, and Tennea Canyon, earning the admiration of Harvard geologist Louis Agassiz, who declared that Muir's glacial observations were the most brilliant in American geological history.

42
00:07:52.240 --> 00:08:07.440
[George] Witnessing the devastating environmental destruction wrought by commercial logging, gold mining, and sheep herds, which Muir bitterly termed hoofed locusts that stripped alpine meadows of all vegetation, Muir embarked on a crusade of public advocacy.

43
00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:22.400
[George] Writing eloquent, passionate articles for Century Magazine alongside editor Robert Underwood Johnson, Muir awakened an urban nation to the beauty of the wild, writing his famous rallying cry climb the mountains and get their good tidings.

44
00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:26.560
[George] Nature's peace will flow into you as sunshine flows into trees.

45
00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:37.840
[George] His relentless campaigning forced Congress to pass legislation creating Yosemite National Park, Sequoia National Park, and General Grant National Park in October 1890.

46
00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:55.040
[George] In June 1892, in a lawyer's office in San Francisco, Muir and a group of wilderness advocates founded the Sierra Club, with Muir serving as its first president until his death, dedicated to exploring, enjoying, and protecting the wild places of the earth.

47
00:08:55.040 --> 00:09:03.240
[George] Muir's crusade to protect Yosemite from the devastating impact of commercial exploitation was driven by a fierce moral urgency.

48
00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:13.880
[George] He watched with heartbreak as commercial logging companies clear cut ancient giant sequoia groves for lumber and sheep herds turned pristine alpine meadows into trampled dust bowls.

49
00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:33.080
[George] Partnering with Century magazine editor Robert Underwood Johnson, Muir unleashed an avalanche of lyrical articles that captivated millions of readers across America, portraying the national parks not as commercial timber reserves, but as sacred outdoor cathedrals essential for the spiritual renewal of urban humanity.

50
00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:47.800
[George] His tireless lobbying forced Congress to establish Yosemite and Sequoia National Parks in 1890, followed by the founding of the Sierra Club in 1892 to serve as an eternal watchdog guarding the wild heritage of the American West.

51
00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:54.280
[George] In May 1903, President Theodore Roosevelt traveled to California with an explicit request.

52
00:09:54.280 --> 00:10:03.240
[George] He wanted to ditch his secret service guards, governors and politicians to spend three uninterrupted days camping alone in Yosemite with John Muir.

53
00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:19.720
[George] Sleeping in wool blankets on beds of pine needles under the majestic canopy of the Mariposa Grove, surrounded by five inches of fresh falling snow, the 65-year-old naturalist and the 44-year-old president talked deep into the night around crackling campfires.

54
00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:27.640
[George] Muir pressed Roosevelt on the sacred duty of the federal government to preserve wild landscapes from timber barons and dam developers.

55
00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:31.080
[George] The experience transformed Roosevelt's presidency.

56
00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:36.440
[George] During his administration, Roosevelt established the United States Forest Service.

57
00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:48.800
[George] Signed the Antiquities Act, and protected 230 million acres of public land, including five national parks, 18 national monuments, and 55 wildlife refuges.

58
00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:59.840
[George] The historic three-day wilderness summit between John Muir and President Theodore Roosevelt in May 1903 was one of the most consequential conservation encounters in world history.

59
00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:15.920
[George] Ditching the presidential entourage and Secret Service guards, the 65-year-old naturalist and the rugged young president rode into Yosemite Valley alone, camping under the ancient giants of the Mariposa Grove and waking covered in four inches of pristine snow.

60
00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:38.880
[George] Around midnight campfires, Muir spoke with passionate eloquence about the urgent moral duty of the presidency to safeguard America's wild landscapes from corporate greed, inspiring Roosevelt to establish the United States Forest Service, sign the Antiquities Act, and permanently protect over 230 million acres of public lands for future generations.

61
00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:54.160
[George] Muir's final years were consumed by the heartbreaking battle to save Hetch Hetchy Valley, a spectacular glacial canyon in Yosemite National Park that the city of San Francisco sought to dam for municipal drinking water following the 1906 earthquake.

62
00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:58.800
[George] Muir fought with heroic fury, writing, Damn Hetch Hetchy.

63
00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:06.880
[George] As well dam for water tanks the people's cathedrals and churches, for no holier temple has ever been consecrated by the heart of man.

64
00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:14.160
[George] When President Woodrow Wilson signed the Raker Act in December 1913, approving the dam, Muir was heartbroken.

65
00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:22.880
[George] Passing away from pneumonia on Christmas Eve 1914 at age 76, Muir died with his manuscript on Alaska by his bedside.

66
00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:31.560
[George] While Hetch Hetchy was lost, the public outrage over its destruction led directly to the creation of the National Park Service in 1916.

67
00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:40.920
[George] John Muir's prophetic voice lives on in every rustling pine, every soaring granite cliff, and every wild heart that hears the call of the mountains.

68
00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:55.000
[George] The tragic battle over Hetch Hetchy Valley was Muir's final, heartbreaking wilderness crusade, pitting his preservationist vision against the utilitarian conservationism of Gifford Pinchot and San Francisco City politicians.

69
00:12:55.000 --> 00:13:08.680
[George] Muir fought with unmatched literary fury to protect Hetch Hetchy's roaring waterfalls and granite domes from being flooded beneath a municipal reservoir, declaring that commercial developers would dam the people's cathedrals for water tanks.

70
00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:22.200
[George] Though Congress approved the dam in 1913, breaking Muir's heart, the nationwide public outrage over Hetch Hetchy's destruction catalyzed the passage of the National Park Service Organic Act of 1916.

71
00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:35.640
[George] John Muir's prophetic spirit remains an undying flame, reminding humanity that the wild places of the earth are sacred temples that restore the soul, calling us forever to climb the mountains and receive their peace.

72
00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:39.240
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

73
00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:49.160
[Rebecca] John Muir's poetic soul, wilderness philosophy, and relentless devotion to preserving wild America created the national parks that safeguard our natural heritage today.

74
00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:53.080
[Rebecca] This concludes our top 50 famous nonpresident American series.

75
00:13:53.080 --> 00:14:02.200
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with our grand new series, The Kings and Queens of England, exploring the astonishing monarchs who forged the British Crown.

76
00:14:02.200 --> 00:14:04.360
[Rebecca] Until then, stay tuned.

